  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Project Scope**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving in to define the scope. My initial focus is grasping the project\'s core goal: a frontend-only prototype for CivicPulse. I\'m prioritizing understanding the dual interface requirements, ensuring I can structure the development accordingly. I\'m sketching out the initial boundaries to clarify the overall project.\n\n\n**Clarifying UI/UX Goals**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining the target audience and establishing the desired \"vibe\" for the interface. I\'ve broken down the UX goals for both the citizen and government user groups, outlining key aspects like cognitive load, reporting ease, and information density. I\'m also zeroing in on the aesthetics, envisioning a clean, professional look with a modern civic-tech feel, a light theme, restrained colors, and specific visual styling. The goal is to maximize trustworthiness and clarity through visual design.\n\n\n**Mapping Out Screen Architectures**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the screen architectures for both citizen and government interfaces, focusing on mapping out the key functionalities. For the citizen side, I\'ve outlined screens for reports, issue details, notifications, and profiles. On the government side, the focus includes overviews, live maps, issue queues, detailed case management, and analytics. I am working on the overall flow, and a portal selector is necessary at the root for switching views.\n\n\n**Planning Component Structures**\n\nI\'m now outlining the technical stack for this prototype. I\'ll stick to a minimal, efficient setup, using React Context or a `useAppState` hook for state management and simple state-based routing. I\'ll mock data in `mockData.ts` for quick setup, and structure components into `citizen` and `government` folders for modularity. I\'ll use `types.ts` for defining data structures. I\'ll keep the dependency list slim, and I\'ll focus on utilities for basic needs. I will also be installing Recharts.\n\n\n**Implementing Core Functionality**\n\nI\'ve just updated `metadata.json` with the application name. I\'m building out the data models, specifically the `Issue` type, including attributes like id, location, and status. I\'m populating mock data with 15-20 issues reflecting real-world scenarios in cities like Chennai and Bengaluru. I\'m focusing on basic state management for issues and building out the router, which will present a landing screen to select between the Citizen App and Government Dashboard. Component-wise, I am focusing on the key layouts for both the citizen app and government dashboard.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Feature Implementation**\n\nI\'m now narrowing down the scope to manage the ambitious feature set. Given the potential token limitations, I\'m streamlining the feature list. For the Citizen App, I\'ll concentrate on the Home, Report Flow, and Issue Detail. For the Government Dashboard, my focus will be on the Overview, Issue Queue, and Issue Detail. To bypass file limits, I\'ll be splitting the code into several files.\n\n\n**Structuring Mobile-First Layouts**\n\nI\'m making the Citizen App mobile-first, and I\'ll constrain it for larger screens. The goal is a mobile app feel, even on desktop, using specific CSS.\n\nI\'m also setting up the necessary tool calls. I\'m installing new dependencies and creating all the files to support core functionalities, splitting up components where required.\n\n\n**Organizing Application Files**\n\nI\'m structuring the codebase now, defining `CitizenApp` for internal routing and sketching the government dashboard with its components and sidebar within `GovApp`. I\'m going to refine the mock data to reflect the CivicPulse brand within the Indian context, specifically in cities like Chennai, using locations like Anna Nagar. Also, I\'m defining a clear theme with colors for various states: blue for accent, red for urgent, green for resolved, etc.\n\nI\'m mapping out the Citizen Report flow (photo, location, details, AI assist, submit), and I am planning out the Gov Case Management Screen\'s layout with evidence, intelligence, and a right panel.\n\n\n**Defining Workflow Structures**\n\nI\'m now integrating the owner, SLA, and assign/resolve button features. I\'m focusing on the bottom-screen elements like the timeline and citizen report. I\'m also planning the concurrency strategy by starting to draft files, starting with `types.ts`, `data.ts`, and `utils.ts`, then the contexts, and the layouts, and finally connecting it all in `App.tsx`. I am going to wait for the compilation.\n\n\n**Refining Data Structures**\n\nI\'m solidifying the data model now, meticulously defining the `Issue` interface with all its attributes, from `id` and `title` to timestamps and location details. I am also working on mock data that I am mapping to realistic city areas in Chennai and Bengaluru, creating data points for context. Next, I am building utilities with `clsx` and `tailwind-merge`. I\'ve decided to use a `Context` provider for mock data, considering the constraints. I will be working on `GovOverview` and `GovLiveMap` screens as well.\n\n\n**Streamlining Screen Implementations**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Gov App features and planning to use tabs for the `GovQueue` and `GovCaseDetail` views to avoid external routing. I\'ll also add an \"Offline Mode\" button in the Citizen app to manage pending reports in a context array. I\'m focusing on mobile-constrained layouts for the Citizen App and a full-screen design for the Gov App, using `lucide-react` for icons. To stay within token limits, I\'ll prioritize detailing the most essential screens while simplifying the secondary ones.\n\n\n"
