steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787827616
      nanos: 380547372
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Add a visible PRIVACY PROTECTION feature to the existing \342\200\234Crowd Sourced Civic Issue Reporting and Resolution\342\200\235 prototype.\n\nDo NOT redesign the application or modify existing functionality.\n\nThe purpose of this feature is to demonstrate that citizen-uploaded photos are automatically processed to protect the privacy of people who may accidentally appear in the image.\n\n==================================================\n1. PHOTO PRIVACY PROCESSING\n==================================================\n\nWhen a citizen uploads or captures a photo:\n\nPhoto\n\342\206\222 Privacy Detection\n\342\206\222 Detect human faces\n\342\206\222 Automatically blur detected faces\n\342\206\222 Store/display the privacy-protected version\n\nThe system should clearly communicate:\n\n\342\200\234Privacy Protected\342\200\235\n\nand:\n\n\342\200\234Faces detected in this image have been automatically blurred to protect personal privacy.\342\200\235\n\n==================================================\n2. CITIZEN REPORT SCREEN\n==================================================\n\nOn the photo preview screen, show the processed image with visible blurred faces.\n\nUnder the image, add a subtle privacy indicator:\n\n\342\234\223 Faces automatically blurred\n\nPrivacy Protection Enabled\n\nAdd a small info icon that opens:\n\n\342\200\234Personal faces detected in uploaded images are automatically blurred before the image is shared with municipal authorities.\342\200\235\n\n==================================================\n3. BEFORE / AFTER DEMONSTRATION\n==================================================\n\nFor the prototype, create a small optional comparison component:\n\nOriginal Image\n\342\206\222\nPrivacy-Protected Image\n\nLabel clearly:\n\n\342\200\234Original\342\200\235\nand\n\342\200\234Faces Blurred\342\200\235\n\nUse realistic mock data.\n\nDo NOT display identifiable faces unnecessarily.\n\nThe blurred version should visibly demonstrate the privacy feature.\n\n==================================================\n4. GOVERNMENT DASHBOARD\n==================================================\n\nWhen municipal staff open an issue containing a citizen photograph, show the privacy-protected image by default.\n\nAdd a small badge:\n\n\342\200\234Privacy Protected\342\200\235\n\nTooltip/info:\n\n\342\200\234Faces detected in citizen-submitted evidence have been automatically blurred.\342\200\235\n\nDo not display the original unblurred image by default.\n\n==================================================\n5. PRIVACY STATUS IN ISSUE DETAILS\n==================================================\n\nInside the government Issue Detail page, add:\n\nPHOTO PRIVACY\n\n\342\234\223 Face detection completed\n\342\234\223 Detected faces blurred\n\342\234\223 Privacy-protected image available\n\nExample:\n\n\342\200\2342 faces automatically blurred\342\200\235\n\nThis should make the privacy mechanism visible to judges.\n\n==================================================\n6. SYSTEM-WIDE PRIVACY MESSAGE\n==================================================\n\nWhere appropriate, include a concise message:\n\n\342\200\234Citizen privacy protected automatically.\342\200\235\n\nDo not use lengthy legal text.\n\n==================================================\n7. DEMO BEHAVIOUR\n==================================================\n\nSimulate the following flow:\n\nCitizen uploads photo\n\342\206\223\nSystem detects faces\n\342\206\223\nFaces automatically blurred\n\342\206\223\nPrivacy-protected image displayed\n\342\206\223\nReport submitted\n\342\206\223\nGovernment sees only the protected image\n\nShow a small processing state:\n\n\342\200\234Protecting privacy\342\200\246\342\200\235\n\nfollowed by:\n\n\342\200\234\342\234\223 Privacy protected\342\200\235\n\n==================================================\n8. IMPORTANT DESIGN REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nKeep this feature subtle and professional.\n\nDo NOT make privacy warnings dominate the interface.\n\nUse:\n- Small shield/privacy icon\n- Clear \342\200\234Privacy Protected\342\200\235 badge\n- Short explanatory text\n- Visible blurred faces in the demo image\n\nThe feature should communicate:\n\n\342\200\234Civic evidence can be shared without unnecessarily exposing bystanders\342\200\231 identities.\342\200\235\n\nDo not change the official product name:\n\n\342\200\234Crowd Sourced Civic Issue Reporting and Resolution\342\200\235\n\nDo not modify the existing authentication, citizen dashboard, government dashboard, workflow, AI features, or visual identity.\n\nOnly add and integrate the privacy-protection experience described above."
    items {
      text: "Add a visible PRIVACY PROTECTION feature to the existing \342\200\234Crowd Sourced Civic Issue Reporting and Resolution\342\200\235 prototype.\n\nDo NOT redesign the application or modify existing functionality.\n\nThe purpose of this feature is to demonstrate that citizen-uploaded photos are automatically processed to protect the privacy of people who may accidentally appear in the image.\n\n==================================================\n1. PHOTO PRIVACY PROCESSING\n==================================================\n\nWhen a citizen uploads or captures a photo:\n\nPhoto\n\342\206\222 Privacy Detection\n\342\206\222 Detect human faces\n\342\206\222 Automatically blur detected faces\n\342\206\222 Store/display the privacy-protected version\n\nThe system should clearly communicate:\n\n\342\200\234Privacy Protected\342\200\235\n\nand:\n\n\342\200\234Faces detected in this image have been automatically blurred to protect personal privacy.\342\200\235\n\n==================================================\n2. CITIZEN REPORT SCREEN\n==================================================\n\nOn the photo preview screen, show the processed image with visible blurred faces.\n\nUnder the image, add a subtle privacy indicator:\n\n\342\234\223 Faces automatically blurred\n\nPrivacy Protection Enabled\n\nAdd a small info icon that opens:\n\n\342\200\234Personal faces detected in uploaded images are automatically blurred before the image is shared with municipal authorities.\342\200\235\n\n==================================================\n3. BEFORE / AFTER DEMONSTRATION\n==================================================\n\nFor the prototype, create a small optional comparison component:\n\nOriginal Image\n\342\206\222\nPrivacy-Protected Image\n\nLabel clearly:\n\n\342\200\234Original\342\200\235\nand\n\342\200\234Faces Blurred\342\200\235\n\nUse realistic mock data.\n\nDo NOT display identifiable faces unnecessarily.\n\nThe blurred version should visibly demonstrate the privacy feature.\n\n==================================================\n4. GOVERNMENT DASHBOARD\n==================================================\n\nWhen municipal staff open an issue containing a citizen photograph, show the privacy-protected image by default.\n\nAdd a small badge:\n\n\342\200\234Privacy Protected\342\200\235\n\nTooltip/info:\n\n\342\200\234Faces detected in citizen-submitted evidence have been automatically blurred.\342\200\235\n\nDo not display the original unblurred image by default.\n\n==================================================\n5. PRIVACY STATUS IN ISSUE DETAILS\n==================================================\n\nInside the government Issue Detail page, add:\n\nPHOTO PRIVACY\n\n\342\234\223 Face detection completed\n\342\234\223 Detected faces blurred\n\342\234\223 Privacy-protected image available\n\nExample:\n\n\342\200\2342 faces automatically blurred\342\200\235\n\nThis should make the privacy mechanism visible to judges.\n\n==================================================\n6. SYSTEM-WIDE PRIVACY MESSAGE\n==================================================\n\nWhere appropriate, include a concise message:\n\n\342\200\234Citizen privacy protected automatically.\342\200\235\n\nDo not use lengthy legal text.\n\n==================================================\n7. DEMO BEHAVIOUR\n==================================================\n\nSimulate the following flow:\n\nCitizen uploads photo\n\342\206\223\nSystem detects faces\n\342\206\223\nFaces automatically blurred\n\342\206\223\nPrivacy-protected image displayed\n\342\206\223\nReport submitted\n\342\206\223\nGovernment sees only the protected image\n\nShow a small processing state:\n\n\342\200\234Protecting privacy\342\200\246\342\200\235\n\nfollowed by:\n\n\342\200\234\342\234\223 Privacy protected\342\200\235\n\n==================================================\n8. IMPORTANT DESIGN REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nKeep this feature subtle and professional.\n\nDo NOT make privacy warnings dominate the interface.\n\nUse:\n- Small shield/privacy icon\n- Clear \342\200\234Privacy Protected\342\200\235 badge\n- Short explanatory text\n- Visible blurred faces in the demo image\n\nThe feature should communicate:\n\n\342\200\234Civic evidence can be shared without unnecessarily exposing bystanders\342\200\231 identities.\342\200\235\n\nDo not change the official product name:\n\n\342\200\234Crowd Sourced Civic Issue Reporting and Resolution\342\200\235\n\nDo not modify the existing authentication, citizen dashboard, government dashboard, workflow, AI features, or visual identity.\n\nOnly add and integrate the privacy-protection experience described above."
    }
  }
