  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing Authentication Challenges**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the authentication complexities within the CSCIRR prototype. The single global authenticated state is proving problematic. Logging out from the Citizen portal, for example, incorrectly terminates the Gov session, and vice-versa.\n\n\n**Reviewing and Revising Flows**\n\nI\'m revisiting the authentication flows, specifically the implementation changes from the prior step. It appears that there are still issues, particularly around the ability to seamlessly switch between the Citizen and Government auth flows. I\'m taking a look at `AppContext.tsx`, `AuthFlow.tsx`, and `App.tsx` to identify and fix the root cause of these switching problems.\n\n\n**Analyzing Authentication States**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the component interactions to pinpoint the authentication issue\'s source. Currently, I\'m examining `App.tsx` and `AppContext.tsx` to get a clearer picture.  Specifically, I\'m looking at how `authState` and `user` are determined and passed down to `MainApp`. If a user logs out from the Citizen portal, the global authentication state is changing, and the `MainApp` is re-rendered to `<AuthFlow />`. The `AuthFlow` state is then updated through calls like \"Demo Municipal Officer\", using `loginGov`, which is setting local storage.\n\n\n**Pinpointing State Synchronization**\n\nI\'m now investigating state synchronization issues stemming from logout scenarios. Specifically, I\'m tracing the implications of logging out from `GovApp`. The `logout` function, when called for \'GOV\', correctly nullifies `govUser` and sets the `authState` to \'NOT_AUTHENTICATED\', triggering a re-render to `<AuthFlow />`. The `AuthFlow` then defaults to `GOV_AUTH` based on `localStorage`. I\'m going to look at how we want to switch to the Citizen portal flow.\n\n\n**Refining Application State Management**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical state management oversight related to application switching. I\'ve realized that modifying `cscirr_active_app` in `AuthFlow` via `localStorage` doesn\'t actually update `activeApp` in `AppContext`. The lack of synchronization means a citizen login attempt after switching back from the gov view would fail because the main app would still be tracking the gov view. I believe `AppContext` needs control over `activeApp` and the auth states, and not just the `AuthFlow`. Exposing these states is now a high priority.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 576779043
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
