  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the State Transition**\n\nI\'m currently focused on a critical bug related to the state transition between GOV and CITIZEN views within the MainApp. It appears the `MainApp` depends on `authState`, which is aliased to an application of the `activeApp`, `citizenAuthState` and the `govUser` boolean to produce an auth state. This introduces a subtle issue when transitioning from GOV to CITIZEN, which I am actively investigating.\n\n\n**Refining the Logout Logic**\n\nI\'ve been re-evaluating the `logout()` function, specifically its role in the \"Switch to Citizen\" flow. The current implementation uses an `if/else` on `activeApp`, which complicates the transition. The goal now is to end the GOV session, clear the current role, and navigate to role selection. I\'m considering setting `activeApp` to a \'NONE\' state post-logout, or perhaps directly to `CITIZEN` if not authenticated. The AuthFlow with WELCOME view seems the destination, as it currently is Role Selection.\n\n\n**Clarifying the View Transition**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical transition scenario in `AuthFlow.tsx`. I see that the `view` state within `<AuthFlow />` is locally managed and defaults to `WELCOME`. The current `logout()` function, when triggered from `GovApp` with the intent to switch to citizen, appears to bypass the role selection screen if the user had previously logged into Citizen. My focus is now on ensuring a consistent journey: logout from the current view, then to the role selection.\n\n\n**Revising the Logout Flow**\n\nI\'m now refining the `logout()` function. The core challenge is the correct handling of session clearing and role unsetting when switching between GOV and CITIZEN. I\'m leaning toward clearing both sessions on logout to ensure Role Selection is always shown, instead of relying on conditionally setting `activeApp` to \'NONE\' post logout. The goal is to fully end the current session, clear both roles, and navigate consistently to role selection. I\'m investigating how best to achieve this within `AppContext.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 26207775
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
