  planner_response {
    response: "I have successfully completed the comprehensive bug fix and data quality update for the \"Crowd Sourced Civic Issue Reporting and Resolution\" prototype. I strictly preserved the existing visual identity, typography, layout, and language system while resolving the functional and consistency issues you highlighted.\n\nHere is a summary of the improvements:\n\n- **Face Blur Removal**: I completely removed the artificial, random circular face-blurring overlays from all evidence photos. The text indicator remains, but the images themselves are now cleanly displayed without incorrect visual artifacts.\n- **Authentication & Role Switching**: The authentication flow and state manager have been deeply refactored. The \"Switch to Citizen\" and \"Switch to Municipal Staff\" buttons are now fully functional, allowing you to seamlessly end the current role session and navigate to the Role Selection screen without refreshing the browser or manually clearing storage. The \"Sign Out\" flows for both Citizen and Government roles work perfectly, securely clearing the active role while preserving the underlying mock dataset and language preferences.\n- **Interactive Government Map**: The GovLiveMap has been upgraded to an interactive `react-leaflet` implementation, strictly bound to the geographic extent of **India**. I introduced a functional toggle allowing the Municipal Staff to switch between the new **Citizen Report Density Heatmap** and standard issue markers. The map directly consumes the primary civic dataset, updating dynamically when filters (like issue type or priority) are applied. \n- **Geographic Consistency & Mock Addresses**: I audited and updated the underlying dataset to ensure geographic consistency. Placeholder addresses like \"Random Street, Delhi\" have been replaced with realistic Indian addresses (e.g., \"Anna Salai\", \"Outer Ring Road\"). I added a `state` field to the schema, ensuring that coordinates, cities, and states perfectly align.\n- **Image Relevance & Consistency**: I fixed the before/after photo mismatch. The image generation logic now ensures that evidence pairs depict the exact same location and infrastructure context to realistically simulate a civic resolution. Furthermore, every mock report now receives a unique, category-relevant image (e.g., actual potholes for pothole reports).\n- **Jurisdiction & Search**: I removed all phrasing suggesting \"Automated AI Jurisdiction Routing\", replacing it with a clean, standard Jurisdiction display block showing Authority, Zone, and Ward data. I also implemented the global search bar in the Government Dashboard, allowing you to instantly filter the map, queue, and overview by Issue ID, city, type, or description.\n- **Export & Branding**: The \"Export Report\" button on the Government Dashboard is now fully operational and downloads the current filtered dataset as a CSV file. The Citizen profile name has been consistently updated to \"Sathyendhar B\", and clicking the main product logo in either dashboard reliably navigates the user back to their respective home overview."
    modified_response: "I have successfully completed the comprehensive bug fix and data quality update for the \"Crowd Sourced Civic Issue Reporting and Resolution\" prototype. I strictly preserved the existing visual identity, typography, layout, and language system while resolving the functional and consistency issues you highlighted.\n\nHere is a summary of the improvements:\n\n- **Face Blur Removal**: I completely removed the artificial, random circular face-blurring overlays from all evidence photos. The text indicator remains, but the images themselves are now cleanly displayed without incorrect visual artifacts.\n- **Authentication & Role Switching**: The authentication flow and state manager have been deeply refactored. The \"Switch to Citizen\" and \"Switch to Municipal Staff\" buttons are now fully functional, allowing you to seamlessly end the current role session and navigate to the Role Selection screen without refreshing the browser or manually clearing storage. The \"Sign Out\" flows for both Citizen and Government roles work perfectly, securely clearing the active role while preserving the underlying mock dataset and language preferences.\n- **Interactive Government Map**: The GovLiveMap has been upgraded to an interactive `react-leaflet` implementation, strictly bound to the geographic extent of **India**. I introduced a functional toggle allowing the Municipal Staff to switch between the new **Citizen Report Density Heatmap** and standard issue markers. The map directly consumes the primary civic dataset, updating dynamically when filters (like issue type or priority) are applied. \n- **Geographic Consistency & Mock Addresses**: I audited and updated the underlying dataset to ensure geographic consistency. Placeholder addresses like \"Random Street, Delhi\" have been replaced with realistic Indian addresses (e.g., \"Anna Salai\", \"Outer Ring Road\"). I added a `state` field to the schema, ensuring that coordinates, cities, and states perfectly align.\n- **Image Relevance & Consistency**: I fixed the before/after photo mismatch. The image generation logic now ensures that evidence pairs depict the exact same location and infrastructure context to realistically simulate a civic resolution. Furthermore, every mock report now receives a unique, category-relevant image (e.g., actual potholes for pothole reports).\n- **Jurisdiction & Search**: I removed all phrasing suggesting \"Automated AI Jurisdiction Routing\", replacing it with a clean, standard Jurisdiction display block showing Authority, Zone, and Ward data. I also implemented the global search bar in the Government Dashboard, allowing you to instantly filter the map, queue, and overview by Issue ID, city, type, or description.\n- **Export & Branding**: The \"Export Report\" button on the Government Dashboard is now fully operational and downloads the current filtered dataset as a CSV file. The Citizen profile name has been consistently updated to \"Sathyendhar B\", and clicking the main product logo in either dashboard reliably navigates the user back to their respective home overview."
