  planner_response {
    response: "I have completed a comprehensive architectural rebuild of the application\'s authentication, role-management, and routing systems. I moved away from internal component-level state rendering and established a deterministic, single-source-of-truth session engine backed by real route boundaries.\n\n### Authentication & Routing Architecture Rebuild\n- **Centralized Auth Manager:** Rebuilt `AppContext.tsx` to serve as the unified source of truth. It explicitly tracks `activeRole`, `citizenSession`, and `governmentSession` with synchronized deterministic states.\n- **Strict Storage Isolation:** Local storage keys are now strictly separated (`civicpulse_citizen_session` and `civicpulse_government_session`). This prevents any possibility of session overlap or role leakage between the Citizen and Government dashboards.\n- **Dedicated Router Namespaces:** Implemented standard React Router mapping for strict boundaries:\n  - `/select-role` serves as the public onboarding gateway.\n  - `/citizen/*` exclusively maps to the Citizen portal.\n  - `/government/*` exclusively maps to the Municipal Staff dashboard.\n- **Hard Protected Route Guards:** Created `CitizenRouteGuard` and `GovernmentRouteGuard`. If a user attempts to access `/citizen/home` without a valid `citizenSession` in memory, the guard immediately replaces the navigation stack and redirects them to the login flow.\n- **Browser Back Resiliency:** All logout and role-switching actions utilize React Router\'s `replace: true` paradigm. This prevents the browser\'s Back button from pulling an authenticated route from history after a session has been terminated.\n- **Symmetric Role Switching:** Integrated `switchToCitizen` and `switchToGov` handlers. These handlers forcefully drop the active role context, clear the specific active session from storage without touching the dormant session, and explicitly route the user back through the secure `AuthFlow`.\n- **Demo Navigation Hatch:** Added a fail-safe \"Exit to Role Selection\" button to both portal sidebars. This guarantees you can cleanly abort any demo session and return to the root role selector.\n\n### Data Preservation\n- **Non-Destructive Session Logging:** Terminating an active session wipes the authentication signature and session context strictly. It intentionally bypasses wiping the core mock datasets, language preferences (`civic_language`), and generated civic issue queues, allowing seamless testing across both roles.\n- **Dev Diagnostics Panel:** I injected a small, semi-transparent Auth Diagnostics panel into the bottom-right of the role selector screen. This provides real-time readout of the global session state to assist in confirming strict state clearance between demos."
    modified_response: "I have completed a comprehensive architectural rebuild of the application\'s authentication, role-management, and routing systems. I moved away from internal component-level state rendering and established a deterministic, single-source-of-truth session engine backed by real route boundaries.\n\n### Authentication & Routing Architecture Rebuild\n- **Centralized Auth Manager:** Rebuilt `AppContext.tsx` to serve as the unified source of truth. It explicitly tracks `activeRole`, `citizenSession`, and `governmentSession` with synchronized deterministic states.\n- **Strict Storage Isolation:** Local storage keys are now strictly separated (`civicpulse_citizen_session` and `civicpulse_government_session`). This prevents any possibility of session overlap or role leakage between the Citizen and Government dashboards.\n- **Dedicated Router Namespaces:** Implemented standard React Router mapping for strict boundaries:\n  - `/select-role` serves as the public onboarding gateway.\n  - `/citizen/*` exclusively maps to the Citizen portal.\n  - `/government/*` exclusively maps to the Municipal Staff dashboard.\n- **Hard Protected Route Guards:** Created `CitizenRouteGuard` and `GovernmentRouteGuard`. If a user attempts to access `/citizen/home` without a valid `citizenSession` in memory, the guard immediately replaces the navigation stack and redirects them to the login flow.\n- **Browser Back Resiliency:** All logout and role-switching actions utilize React Router\'s `replace: true` paradigm. This prevents the browser\'s Back button from pulling an authenticated route from history after a session has been terminated.\n- **Symmetric Role Switching:** Integrated `switchToCitizen` and `switchToGov` handlers. These handlers forcefully drop the active role context, clear the specific active session from storage without touching the dormant session, and explicitly route the user back through the secure `AuthFlow`.\n- **Demo Navigation Hatch:** Added a fail-safe \"Exit to Role Selection\" button to both portal sidebars. This guarantees you can cleanly abort any demo session and return to the root role selector.\n\n### Data Preservation\n- **Non-Destructive Session Logging:** Terminating an active session wipes the authentication signature and session context strictly. It intentionally bypasses wiping the core mock datasets, language preferences (`civic_language`), and generated civic issue queues, allowing seamless testing across both roles.\n- **Dev Diagnostics Panel:** I injected a small, semi-transparent Auth Diagnostics panel into the bottom-right of the role selector screen. This provides real-time readout of the global session state to assist in confirming strict state clearance between demos."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
