  planner_response {
    response: "I have implemented the heatmap exactly as requested based on the visual reference you provided. \n\nHere are the changes that have been deployed to the preview:\n\n1. **Custom Heatmap Gradient:** I modified the `leaflet.heat` gradient configuration to perfectly match the color scale in your screenshot (ranging from pale yellow/cream for `0.0` through bright oranges and reds, scaling up to very dark brown/black for `20+`).\n2. **Data Point Spreading:** I increased the core `radius` and `blur` parameters of the heatmap rendering engine so that the points fuse together into large, continuous regional heat blobs rather than small isolated dots, matching the macro-scale look of the reference map.\n3. **Targeted City Seeding:** I updated the underlying geographic mock data engine (`src/data.ts`) to heavily weight reports in the exact cities highlighted in your screenshot (Ludhiana, Meerut, New Delhi, Faribabad, Gwalior, Kota, Surat, Mumbai, and Bengaluru). \n4. **City Overlay Labels:** When viewing the heatmap, a custom map layer will overlay white circle markers and high-contrast text labels (with black text-shadow borders) for those specific cities to replicate the exact visual annotations from your image.\n5. **Interactive Legend:** I added a fixed, floating CSS legend to the bottom of the map that visually maps the 8 color stops (from 0.0 to 20) as shown in the screenshot.\n\nTo view it:\nSign in to the **Municipal Staff (Government Portal)**, open the sidebar, and navigate to **\"Recurring Hotspots\"** (or the general Map view). The heatmap mode will render automatically."
